---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const dsysCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Tokens: design decisions as data', note: 'Colour, spacing, type scales, radii named once (--colour-accent, --space-4) and referenced everywhere — change the token, change the product. Tokens are the design lesson\'s CSS variables: single source of truth (the topics.json of a design).' },
    { math: '2 · Components: the Lego kit', note: 'Buttons, inputs, cards, modals — built once with all states (the interaction lesson), documented, versioned. Every page composes components; the system is a LANGUAGE: grammar (rules) plus vocabulary (components).' },
    { math: '3 · Documentation is the product', note: 'A system nobody can find or understand is a folder of good intentions: usage guidance (when NOT to use), live playground code, contribution process. The writing lesson\'s clarity discipline applies — a system doc is technical writing with screenshots.' },
    { math: '4 · Governance: the human problem', note: 'Adoption beats completeness: one team actually using 20 components beats 200 nobody uses. Contribution models (central team vs federated), versioning discipline (semver for components), and the political work of making consistency cheaper than divergence (the scaling lesson\'s design counterpart).' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/design/advanced/design-systems">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Design systems are <strong>the systems-engineering lesson applied to design itself</strong> —
    tokens, components and governance that let a hundred designers and developers ship one
    coherent product. This site you\'re reading runs on exactly this logic (its own CSS tokens
    and shared components).
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="tokens, components, documentation, governance" widget="equationStep" config={dsysCfg} />

  <h2>The known outcomes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Speed with consistency:</strong> the documented result — teams ship screens faster (composing beats reinventing) and every surface inherits fixes; accessibility fixes applied once, deployed everywhere (the accessibility lesson\'s payoff at scale).</li>
    <li><strong>The big public systems teach the craft:</strong> Material (Google), Human Interface Guidelines (Apple), Polaris (Shopify), Carbon (IBM) — each encodes a platform\'s interaction philosophy; reading their component documentation is a free design education (the component API is the argument).</li>
    <li><strong>The rigidity trap:</strong> systems calcify when contributions are slow or the central team becomes a bottleneck — the balance (guardrails for coherence, escape hatches for innovation) is the strategy lesson\'s standardisation-flexibility trade at design scale.</li>
    <li><strong>Multi-brand systems generalise further:</strong> theming (tokens swapped per brand) runs white-label products — the token layer is what made design systems a business asset, not just a consistency tool.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Figma/Sketch system case studies; the Sparkbox design-system survey; the systems-engineering and typography lessons.</p>
</TopicPage>
